1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Blasted by dynamite and carved by mechanical titans deep beneath the Earth's surface.

2
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Huge underground caverns and cathedrals used to store everything from classic films to secret documents.

3
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:24,000
From them we extract a crystal that's vital to our very existence.

4
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, Salt Mines on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:43,001
Salt.

6
00:00:44,840 --> 00:00:47,960
It's a simple substance, as ordinary as it is plentiful.

7
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:55,960
But it's an invaluable resource with more than 14,000 uses.

8
00:00:55,961 --> 00:01:02,500
from de-icing our roads to softening our water.

9
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:08,180
It's within each and every one of us,

10
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:11,080
an indispensable part of our biological chemistry.

11
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:16,840
And it stands alone as the only rock that we eat.

12
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:22,160
There's a tremendous amount of salt in the Earth,

13
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:25,960
especially in the United States.

14
00:01:25,961 --> 00:01:28,700
Eons in the past, North America was covered

15
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:30,900
by prehistoric seas.

16
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,600
150 million years ago, when the seas evaporated,

17
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:38,200
they left vast beds of salt, salt that was eventually

18
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:39,700
covered by layers of sediment.

19
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:46,180
Part of the enormous deposit lies 1,800 feet directly

20
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:48,720
beneath Lake Erie.

21
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:51,720
Workers at Cargill De-Icing Technologies Salt Mine

22
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:54,120
in Cleveland, Ohio are busy tapping it.

23
00:01:54,121 --> 00:01:58,120
The salt that we're mining today is 400 million years old.

24
00:01:58,121 --> 00:02:02,120
And our mine itself is out underneath the lake.

25
00:02:02,121 --> 00:02:04,120
How do you keep the lake out of the mine?

26
00:02:04,121 --> 00:02:07,120
We've got 1,200-foot of limestone that protects the mine

27
00:02:07,121 --> 00:02:09,120
from the lake bottom.

28
00:02:09,121 --> 00:02:12,120
We actually go out three miles offshore,

29
00:02:12,121 --> 00:02:15,120
and we extend across the face of the city of Cleveland

30
00:02:15,121 --> 00:02:18,120
almost six miles.

31
00:02:18,121 --> 00:02:20,120
The Cleveland mine, like all modern salt mines,

32
00:02:20,121 --> 00:02:23,120
thunders with a constant barrage of precision exploration.

33
00:02:23,121 --> 00:02:26,560
precision explosives, blasting out millions of tons of salt

34
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:27,560
each year.

35
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:35,060
Nearly half of the salt produced in the United States

36
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:36,360
is quarried in this way.

37
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:42,900
The workers who make it all happen

38
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,900
begin by taking a ride from the surface

39
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,280
to the vast subterranean chamber below.

40
00:02:48,380 --> 00:02:51,340
The service cage is used for transporting personnel

41
00:02:51,341 --> 00:02:53,740
and supplies into the mine.

42
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:58,140
We're going down 1,800 feet, dropping at 500 feet a minute.

43
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:00,640
And it will take us approximately three and a half

44
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:01,880
minutes to get to the bottom.

45
00:03:05,220 --> 00:03:07,720
The subterranean world at the bottom of the shaft

46
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:09,520
takes 25 minutes to traverse.

47
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:14,940
A network of tunnels links chambers

48
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:18,740
excavated in the last half century.

49
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:21,100
Pillars of pure salt support the roof of each

50
00:03:21,101 --> 00:03:23,300
of each cavernous room.

51
00:03:23,301 --> 00:03:26,440
Behind me, you see the room and pillar method

52
00:03:26,441 --> 00:03:28,380
that we utilize here.

53
00:03:28,381 --> 00:03:33,620
This pillar is 30 feet wide, 20 feet thick, and 20 feet tall.

54
00:03:33,621 --> 00:03:37,620
It's solid salt that we leave behind to help support

55
00:03:37,621 --> 00:03:39,920
the ground conditions here in the mine.

56
00:03:39,921 --> 00:03:44,120
Helps create a safe work environment for the employees.

57
00:03:44,121 --> 00:03:46,760
The sturdy pillars prevent everything up there

58
00:03:46,761 --> 00:03:49,960
from collapsing down here, enabling workers

59
00:03:49,961 --> 00:03:53,960
of extracting 10,000 tons of salt from the mine every day.

60
00:03:56,700 --> 00:03:59,200
A crewman operating a machine called a scaler

61
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:00,400
starts the process.

62
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:05,100
He preps a chamber for the blasting team

63
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:08,420
by scraping away loose chunks of salt from the roof and walls.

64
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:16,960
Next, a rig called a bolter inserts metal rods

65
00:04:16,961 --> 00:04:21,960
into the chamber roof at strategic spots.

66
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:24,960
This compresses the rock, helping the ceiling to remain

67
00:04:25,060 --> 00:04:28,900
intact as the face of the wall is detonated.

68
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,400
The bolter installs 10 rods, each capable of anchoring

69
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:36,420
about 25,000 pounds. This is a six-foot bolt,

70
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:40,320
three-quarter inch rod iron. Once the drill's hold, we take

71
00:04:40,420 --> 00:04:43,760
resin, basically for anchoring the bolt, stick the resin in

72
00:04:43,761 --> 00:04:47,360
put a six-by-six plate on top of the bolt.

73
00:04:47,361 --> 00:04:51,700
After the bolter finishes prepping the roof,

74
00:04:51,701 --> 00:04:54,800
a machine called a smag operator starts prepping the wall

75
00:04:54,801 --> 00:04:56,100
to be blasted.

76
00:04:56,101 --> 00:05:02,340
A smag operator is a machine that has three 11-inch auger

77
00:05:02,341 --> 00:05:04,640
drills that are carbide-tipped.

78
00:05:04,641 --> 00:05:09,020
They drill approximately 24 feet deep.

79
00:05:09,021 --> 00:05:11,760
The machine drills six holes in all.

80
00:05:11,761 --> 00:05:14,600
They'll remain empty to leave room for the exploding salt

81
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:15,600
to expand.

82
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:21,800
Next, workers drill dozens of additional holes,

83
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:24,700
just as deep but only 2 inches wide.

84
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:26,800
These will house the explosive charges.

85
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:29,420
We're drilling 50 holes per face,

86
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:33,920
and we have to drill in a certain pattern.

87
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:38,860
Basically, we're 4 1⁄2 feet from each hole.

88
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:41,760
Next, the powder crew takes over.

89
00:05:41,761 --> 00:05:44,600
loading each of the 50 holes with ammonium nitrate,

90
00:05:44,601 --> 00:05:47,100
or AMFOAM.

91
00:05:47,101 --> 00:05:49,800
We have the AMFOAM put in a pot.

92
00:05:49,801 --> 00:05:52,800
We have 50 caps and 50 boosters.

93
00:05:52,801 --> 00:05:54,800
Put your booster on your cap first,

94
00:05:54,801 --> 00:05:56,800
and then once you put the caps in,

95
00:05:56,801 --> 00:05:59,420
you put your AMFOAM in, which is in a hose.

96
00:05:59,421 --> 00:06:01,880
Basically, you're packing the AMFOAM into the hole

97
00:06:01,881 --> 00:06:03,180
to make sure it's tight.

98
00:06:03,181 --> 00:06:04,880
So when it detonates, it's a tight explosion,

99
00:06:04,881 --> 00:06:07,880
and it blows out every piece of salt.

100
00:06:07,881 --> 00:06:11,760
The center of this room, which we refer to as the burn,

101
00:06:11,761 --> 00:06:14,160
We'll shoot first, and then the rest of the room

102
00:06:14,260 --> 00:06:18,340
will shoot towards the center on a delayed basis.

103
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:21,340
When it is completed, we'll have anywhere from 800

104
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:22,780
to 1,000 tons on the ground.

105
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:29,280
Once the fuse is tied on, the shock crew will come down,

106
00:06:29,380 --> 00:06:32,780
light the fuse, and they'll leave the unit.

107
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:35,980
16 hours of demanding work now boils down

108
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:37,280
to one critical instant.

109
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:46,760
After the dust clears, it's showtime for this mechanical

110
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:51,700
brute, an Elphinstone Loader, or mucker.

111
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:55,700
Scoop by scoop, it begins the grunt work of moving dislodged

112
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:57,300
salt from the mine.

113
00:06:57,400 --> 00:07:01,240
I pull in there, and I pick up a bucket load of salt,

114
00:07:01,340 --> 00:07:05,780
which is an average of 20 tons per bucket.

115
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:09,660
And then I'll take and I'll haul it around to our crusher.

116
00:07:09,661 --> 00:07:13,460
We call it a stammler, but it crushes up the solvent

117
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:14,940
to smaller pieces.

118
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:17,560
And then I'll come around, and I'll get another load,

119
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:21,240
and I'll just keep repeating this until I empty

120
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:22,980
this whole section out.

121
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:29,180
The Elphinstone loader and all the heavy machinery here

122
00:07:29,280 --> 00:07:32,880
was lowered into the mine in pieces and then reassembled.

123
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:37,660
None of the multimillion-dollar rigs

124
00:07:37,661 --> 00:07:39,900
will ever emerge from the mine.

125
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:42,760
Once a vehicle is taken out of service,

126
00:07:42,860 --> 00:07:44,460
we have what we call a boneyard.

127
00:07:44,560 --> 00:07:46,060
We take the old equipment out there

128
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:49,400
and we'll scavenge any parts off it that we can use

129
00:07:49,500 --> 00:07:50,900
on other pieces of equipment.

130
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:57,320
Otherwise, it's stored out there forever.

131
00:07:57,420 --> 00:07:59,440
A date with a boneyard is still years

132
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:01,980
away for the Stamler, or feeder breaker,

133
00:08:02,080 --> 00:08:04,460
that crushes the salt from the recent blast site

134
00:08:04,461 --> 00:08:08,960
into softball-sized chunks. It keeps the mining process moving

135
00:08:08,961 --> 00:08:11,960
by dumping the salt onto a conveyor that leads to a

136
00:08:11,961 --> 00:08:15,340
subterranean mill. It runs approximately 18 hours

137
00:08:15,341 --> 00:08:20,840
a day, 750 tons an hour. From here, it goes up to our main

138
00:08:20,841 --> 00:08:23,340
crushers. It's crushed, goes through the

139
00:08:23,341 --> 00:08:28,080
screen plant, gets screened to product size.

140
00:08:28,081 --> 00:08:31,920
Now the screen salt has to make a prodigious 1,800-foot

141
00:08:31,921 --> 00:08:35,060
100-foot ascent to the surface.

142
00:08:35,160 --> 00:08:37,720
That journey comes courtesy of this brawny production

143
00:08:37,820 --> 00:08:39,660
elevator, or SKIP.

144
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:41,560
Basically, it's a load bucket.

145
00:08:41,660 --> 00:08:43,460
It carries 20 tons of salt.

146
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:46,000
When this one is up dumping, the second one

147
00:08:46,100 --> 00:08:47,660
is down in the bottom loading.

148
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:48,980
And every couple of minutes, they

149
00:08:49,080 --> 00:08:53,780
change places to bring up the salt every day.

150
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:57,220
Each skip weighs about 50,000 pounds empty.

151
00:08:57,320 --> 00:09:00,620
They hang from four ropes that go from the top of one skip

152
00:09:00,621 --> 00:09:04,120
and over the hoist drum and back down to the other skip.

153
00:09:04,220 --> 00:09:06,360
The total combined weight hanging on the drum

154
00:09:06,460 --> 00:09:09,620
is over 100,000 pounds.

155
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:11,900
This ongoing parade of salt is propelled

156
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,140
by twin electric motors, producing 3,000 horsepower.

157
00:09:18,600 --> 00:09:22,140
The skip runs two shifts per day, hauling up 20 tons of salt

158
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:23,240
every two minutes.

159
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:25,340
You can see the door opens and unloads

160
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:28,840
that 20 tons of salt in just a matter of seconds.

161
00:09:28,841 --> 00:09:31,740
And it carries a price tag of a quarter million dollars.

162
00:09:31,840 --> 00:09:33,580
This is our cash cow.

163
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:36,080
Nothing in mining is cheap, and this is no exception.

164
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:40,380
But we expect this to last us another 14, 15 years.

165
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:42,880
From here, the salt hitches a ride on a conveyor

166
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:45,320
to a central holding facility.

167
00:09:45,420 --> 00:09:47,820
The conveyor belt comes up here to the fifth floor bin

168
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:50,900
house, where this system known as a tripper distributes

169
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,800
that salt to the 36 bins you see here.

170
00:09:54,900 --> 00:09:58,040
The control of this tripper is from the control room

171
00:09:58,041 --> 00:09:59,540
you see up above.

172
00:09:59,640 --> 00:10:01,840
The operator up there has a choice to move the tripper

173
00:10:01,940 --> 00:10:04,020
left and right and forward and backward

174
00:10:04,120 --> 00:10:07,820
in order to dump it into any one of these bins.

175
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:10,980
Next, the salt rides conveyors to a variety of loading docks.

176
00:10:13,560 --> 00:10:16,420
Workers will load 1 million tons into trucks,

177
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:20,100
another million into boats, and another half million tons

178
00:10:20,200 --> 00:10:21,200
will leave by train.

179
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:26,160
Rail cars awaiting their allotment line up

180
00:10:26,161 --> 00:10:28,800
line up in strings of 10.

181
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:33,400
And for a car like this, there's 25, 30 batches

182
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:34,800
to make up the car.

183
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:37,700
So we just, you know, boom, boom, and that's what you'll see.

184
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:39,000
Boom, boom, boom, boom.

185
00:10:39,100 --> 00:10:40,880
Every time you see a splash of salt,

186
00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:43,340
it's about 5,000 pounds of salt.

187
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:48,520
These cars service primarily the middle parts of the Midwest

188
00:10:48,620 --> 00:10:52,280
states, Ohio, Indiana, northern Kentucky to some extent,

189
00:10:52,380 --> 00:10:53,820
and all the way over to Baltimore.

190
00:10:56,160 --> 00:10:58,660
A lot of this salt will end up as a de-icing agent

191
00:10:58,760 --> 00:11:01,500
on the snowy roads of the Midwest.

192
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:04,940
60,000 to 70,000 tons alone will stay local,

193
00:11:05,040 --> 00:11:06,800
sprinkled across the streets of Cleveland.

194
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:13,380
Over the centuries, we've discovered countless ways

195
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:16,720
to use salt, but not all of it has been extracted from deep

196
00:11:16,820 --> 00:11:19,420
within the earth.

197
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:22,780
A treasure of salt awaits harvesting just a few tantalizing

198
00:11:22,880 --> 00:11:26,160
inches below the sea.

199
00:11:26,161 --> 00:11:33,160
Louisiana's Avery Island salt mine, discovered in 1862,

200
00:11:33,260 --> 00:11:36,160
is the oldest in the Western Hemisphere.

201
00:11:40,180 --> 00:11:44,080
Salt, the general of foods, as one ancient writer described

202
00:11:44,180 --> 00:11:47,520
it, is usually thought of as something we sprinkle on meals.

203
00:11:49,720 --> 00:11:52,480
But salt is much more than that.

204
00:11:52,481 --> 00:11:56,720
Just 4% of the 50 million tons of salt produced in the US

205
00:11:56,820 --> 00:11:58,420
each year is eaten.

206
00:12:00,520 --> 00:12:03,620
The chemical properties of salt help prevent our household

207
00:12:03,720 --> 00:12:08,460
pipes from clogging by changing hard water to soft water.

208
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:11,460
One of the industrial uses that we often don't appreciate

209
00:12:11,560 --> 00:12:14,800
is ion exchange water softening, where we take

210
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:18,140
hardness chemicals, calcium and magnesium, out of the water.

211
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:21,540
And by exchanging the sodium ion for the calcium and magnesium

212
00:12:21,541 --> 00:12:25,240
magnesium ions, you make a softened water, which sudses,

213
00:12:25,340 --> 00:12:28,340
which is much more useful and consumer-friendly,

214
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:33,280
so you have some shine in your hair after you take a shampoo.

215
00:12:33,380 --> 00:12:35,280
Most of the salt in the world is consumed

216
00:12:35,380 --> 00:12:37,680
by the chemical industry.

217
00:12:37,780 --> 00:12:41,100
Processing plants split salt into its component elements,

218
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:44,100
sodium and chloride, which are used to make everything

219
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:50,300
from plastics to detergent to poison gas.

220
00:12:50,301 --> 00:12:53,500
Historians believe humans first discovered salt's value

221
00:12:53,600 --> 00:12:55,700
when primitive tribes followed animal herds

222
00:12:55,800 --> 00:12:59,100
to natural salt licks.

223
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:01,640
As civilizations developed, cultures

224
00:13:01,740 --> 00:13:04,140
learned that they could produce salt by boiling sea

225
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:06,540
or spring water until only the salt remained.

226
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:12,160
The ancient Romans exemplified the degree to which humanity

227
00:13:12,260 --> 00:13:14,860
valued this simple mineral.

228
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:17,920
They named one of their most important imperial roads

229
00:13:17,921 --> 00:13:20,560
the Via Salaria, or salt road.

230
00:13:20,660 --> 00:13:22,560
Their appreciation of salt was also reflected

231
00:13:22,660 --> 00:13:24,120
in their language.

232
00:13:24,220 --> 00:13:28,300
If you look at the word salary, you can see right there

233
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:29,540
the root word.

234
00:13:29,640 --> 00:13:31,640
It comes from the word sal, salt.

235
00:13:31,740 --> 00:13:36,000
And Roman soldiers were often paid a portion of their salary

236
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:38,500
in salt if they were out in battle

237
00:13:38,600 --> 00:13:40,340
because they had to have salt.

238
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:42,440
And so everywhere you look in human history,

239
00:13:42,540 --> 00:13:45,440
you see salt having that sort of value.

240
00:13:45,441 --> 00:13:47,840
of value.

241
00:13:47,940 --> 00:13:50,740
Salt mined from the Sahara's ancient dry seabeds

242
00:13:50,840 --> 00:13:52,540
powered a lucrative trade in Africa.

243
00:13:55,780 --> 00:13:59,080
Merchants exchanged it for gold offered by salt-hungry cultures

244
00:13:59,180 --> 00:14:03,300
south of the desert. This silent trade, as it was called,

245
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:09,600
still continues in Ethiopia. In 1930, Mohandas Gandhi

246
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:12,460
used salt to rally the Indian people during his struggle

247
00:14:12,461 --> 00:14:16,060
to free them from British colonial rule.

248
00:14:16,160 --> 00:14:19,060
He staged a 240-mile march to the sea

249
00:14:19,160 --> 00:14:23,100
to protest Britain's salt tax, as well as its law banning

250
00:14:23,200 --> 00:14:26,000
Indians from producing salt.

251
00:14:26,100 --> 00:14:28,500
He and people with him marched out into the sea,

252
00:14:28,600 --> 00:14:30,940
and he came back out of the sea and picked up

253
00:14:31,040 --> 00:14:32,580
a fistful of salt.

254
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:34,820
And people all over India, Indians,

255
00:14:34,920 --> 00:14:36,520
started picking up salt.

256
00:14:36,620 --> 00:14:37,960
And it was chaos.

257
00:14:38,060 --> 00:14:40,920
And thousands and thousands of people went to jail.

258
00:14:40,921 --> 00:14:42,920
And they went to jail for touching the salt.

259
00:14:46,420 --> 00:14:50,160
Today, our relationship with salt remains as strong as ever.

260
00:14:50,260 --> 00:14:53,960
We need it physiologically, a minimum of three grams a day,

261
00:14:54,060 --> 00:14:55,860
three pounds a year.

262
00:14:55,960 --> 00:14:59,640
Salt is necessary because both the sodium and the chloride

263
00:14:59,740 --> 00:15:01,040
have essential functions.

264
00:15:01,140 --> 00:15:03,380
The sodium, for example, is engaged

265
00:15:03,480 --> 00:15:05,880
in transporting electrical impulses

266
00:15:05,980 --> 00:15:08,280
from inside the cell to outside the cell.

267
00:15:08,281 --> 00:15:10,920
our bodies are really electrical machines,

268
00:15:11,020 --> 00:15:13,720
and salt is what makes that happen.

269
00:15:13,820 --> 00:15:16,680
Since our very lives depend on this simple substance,

270
00:15:16,780 --> 00:15:19,120
it's not surprising that we've devised a number of ways

271
00:15:19,220 --> 00:15:20,220
to procure it.

272
00:15:24,120 --> 00:15:28,540
Solution mining dates back to the 19th century.

273
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:31,000
Workers begin the process by drilling a pipe

274
00:15:31,100 --> 00:15:33,940
into a subterranean deposit.

275
00:15:34,040 --> 00:15:37,380
They shoot water down the pipe, creating an underground pool

276
00:15:37,381 --> 00:15:40,620
pool of salt-intense brine.

277
00:15:40,720 --> 00:15:43,380
Then pumps force the brine up a second pipe,

278
00:15:43,480 --> 00:15:47,020
and boilers heat it until nothing but pure salt remains.

279
00:15:47,120 --> 00:15:50,120
That salt is used for food grade, water softening

280
00:15:50,220 --> 00:15:53,120
pellets, animal feed, other types of chemical processing

281
00:15:53,220 --> 00:15:56,640
and manufacturing.

282
00:15:56,740 --> 00:15:58,640
Another method is through solar evaporation,

283
00:15:58,740 --> 00:16:02,280
by bringing in seawater or salty brine water,

284
00:16:02,380 --> 00:16:04,740
allowing it to collect in ponds in the sun

285
00:16:04,840 --> 00:16:07,380
and let the sun and wind evaporate the water off.

286
00:16:07,381 --> 00:16:12,220
They call it salt farming, and Cargill's 1,000 acres of salt

287
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:15,680
farms in the San Francisco Bay Area demonstrate that it's a

288
00:16:15,780 --> 00:16:18,020
method as colorful as it is effective.

289
00:16:18,120 --> 00:16:22,420
Well, watching salt being produced is magical, and salt

290
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:25,800
in beautiful colors, especially red salt.

291
00:16:25,900 --> 00:16:28,900
It's just so gorgeous to be in that environment.

292
00:16:31,100 --> 00:16:33,140
Lots of sunshine and steady winds

293
00:16:33,240 --> 00:16:36,440
combine with the bay's salty water to produce 600,000

294
00:16:36,441 --> 00:16:38,100
tons of salt per year.

295
00:16:41,140 --> 00:16:42,740
Our harvest is in the fall.

296
00:16:42,840 --> 00:16:46,180
Like a normal food crop, we grow the salt

297
00:16:46,280 --> 00:16:49,080
during the summer months.

298
00:16:49,180 --> 00:16:52,820
What we have here, this is the salt, the washed salt going

299
00:16:52,920 --> 00:16:55,000
onto our stack.

300
00:16:55,100 --> 00:16:58,100
The new crop is in front, and last year's crop, which we're

301
00:16:58,200 --> 00:17:01,600
reclaiming, is behind it.

302
00:17:01,700 --> 00:17:04,500
The farming process begins when highly concentrated

303
00:17:04,501 --> 00:17:08,340
saltwater, or brine, is pumped into 70-acre crystallization

304
00:17:08,440 --> 00:17:11,600
ponds like this one.

305
00:17:11,700 --> 00:17:15,140
Next, just add time, lots of it.

306
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:19,020
What I have in my hand here are a number of crystals of salt

307
00:17:19,120 --> 00:17:20,820
that have grown together.

308
00:17:20,920 --> 00:17:23,980
Five years ago, they were sodium and chloride ions

309
00:17:24,080 --> 00:17:26,920
floating around out in San Francisco Bay.

310
00:17:27,020 --> 00:17:29,400
And we pump the bay water into our system,

311
00:17:29,500 --> 00:17:31,220
into our concentrators.

312
00:17:31,320 --> 00:17:33,060
And we pump it around for five years

313
00:17:33,061 --> 00:17:36,560
until it's concentrated enough to grow, crop the salt.

314
00:17:36,561 --> 00:17:38,900
It looks like it's going to be a good harvest this year.

315
00:17:38,901 --> 00:17:42,600
The crystals are large, and that's a sign of a good crop.

316
00:17:42,601 --> 00:17:45,140
The Bay Area is ideal for salt production

317
00:17:45,141 --> 00:17:48,200
because of all these strong winds that

318
00:17:48,201 --> 00:17:50,640
come usually after noon.

319
00:17:50,641 --> 00:17:54,140
And this will help evaporation a lot

320
00:17:54,141 --> 00:17:57,020
and will mix all your components of your bride

321
00:17:57,021 --> 00:18:01,020
and increase the salt crystallization.

322
00:18:03,060 --> 00:18:05,820
Salt-loving bacteria also accelerate the evaporation

323
00:18:05,920 --> 00:18:07,400
process.

324
00:18:07,500 --> 00:18:09,660
These plentiful bacteria, along with millions

325
00:18:09,760 --> 00:18:12,000
of tiny single-celled plants, also

326
00:18:12,100 --> 00:18:15,500
infuse the brine with its signature color.

327
00:18:15,600 --> 00:18:19,300
The color of the brine is given by different organisms.

328
00:18:19,400 --> 00:18:23,520
Usually, we have the red, the orange, and the pink.

329
00:18:23,620 --> 00:18:27,760
And that will be dependent on what kind of either bacteria

330
00:18:27,860 --> 00:18:32,260
or green, blue-green ologies.

331
00:18:32,261 --> 00:18:35,560
Biologists constantly monitor the salinity of the brine,

332
00:18:35,660 --> 00:18:37,640
which must remain within a narrow spectrum

333
00:18:37,740 --> 00:18:41,000
to produce the highest quality salt.

334
00:18:41,100 --> 00:18:43,240
Left unchecked, the microorganisms

335
00:18:43,340 --> 00:18:47,000
can make the brine too salty, and an overabundance of rain

336
00:18:47,100 --> 00:18:47,940
can dilute the brine.

337
00:18:50,840 --> 00:18:53,740
When the crop is mature, the exposed salt bed is five

338
00:18:53,840 --> 00:18:54,780
to seven inches thick.

339
00:18:58,960 --> 00:19:01,120
After a grater breaks up the salt,

340
00:19:01,121 --> 00:19:03,860
a mechanical harvester with a snowplow-like blade

341
00:19:03,960 --> 00:19:07,720
scoops up the pieces.

342
00:19:07,820 --> 00:19:10,420
Once on the harvester, conveyors transfer the salt

343
00:19:10,520 --> 00:19:13,060
to cargo trucks.

344
00:19:13,160 --> 00:19:16,260
They'll finish gathering the crop in three months, just in time

345
00:19:16,360 --> 00:19:19,580
to beat the winter rains. You can see it's kind of a dance.

346
00:19:19,680 --> 00:19:22,380
They have to go along at almost the same speed

347
00:19:22,480 --> 00:19:27,620
as the harvester. The truck's being loaded at about 1,500

348
00:19:27,621 --> 00:19:32,620
It'll take a little over a minute to load that 30-ton truck.

349
00:19:32,621 --> 00:19:37,620
From here, the truck drives about a mile to our washer,

350
00:19:37,621 --> 00:19:42,620
and it turns from that brownish color to pure white.

351
00:19:42,621 --> 00:19:44,620
This is our salt washer.

352
00:19:44,621 --> 00:19:47,620
The salt comes in at about 95% purity.

353
00:19:47,621 --> 00:19:50,620
When it's done washing, what we see here is near the exit.

354
00:19:50,621 --> 00:19:54,620
It's about 99% sodium chloride.

355
00:19:54,621 --> 00:20:00,620
From here, it gets sprayed again with a clean, fresh water rinse.

356
00:20:00,621 --> 00:20:05,620
And over here, the salt is exiting our screens

357
00:20:05,621 --> 00:20:12,620
and going on the conveyor belts onto our big salt stack on the right.

358
00:20:12,621 --> 00:20:16,620
This mound is what we've harvested in the last week,

359
00:20:16,621 --> 00:20:20,620
and it's the cleanest solar salt in the world.

360
00:20:20,621 --> 00:20:22,620
And I love looking at that pile.

361
00:20:22,621 --> 00:20:26,620
I could look at that pile for hours.

362
00:20:26,720 --> 00:20:29,820
Salt's glistening purity is showcased magnificently

363
00:20:29,920 --> 00:20:35,060
in this illustrious setting, the strange subterranean world

364
00:20:35,160 --> 00:20:36,560
of Wiolicka, Poland.

365
00:20:41,080 --> 00:20:43,880
The ocean provides us with the largest resource of salt

366
00:20:43,980 --> 00:20:45,280
in the world.

367
00:20:45,380 --> 00:20:49,180
One cubic kilometer of seawater contains 78 million metric

368
00:20:49,280 --> 00:20:51,380
tons of salt.

369
00:20:52,620 --> 00:20:57,620
This illustrious chapel, one of the oldest and most beautiful

370
00:20:57,720 --> 00:21:01,220
in Poland, is packed with precious relics, carvings,

371
00:21:01,320 --> 00:21:03,860
and works of art.

372
00:21:03,960 --> 00:21:07,800
But there's something strange here, something that

373
00:21:07,900 --> 00:21:09,440
makes this chapel different.

374
00:21:12,580 --> 00:21:18,420
The Blessed Kinga Chapel lies 300 feet underground, carved

375
00:21:18,520 --> 00:21:22,620
in the depths of a medieval salt mine.

376
00:21:22,621 --> 00:21:28,820
Everything in it, the walls, floor, chandeliers, altar,

377
00:21:28,920 --> 00:21:33,860
and works of art, was crafted by miners from pure salt.

378
00:21:33,960 --> 00:21:38,940
Visitors can see many reliefs, beautiful reliefs and figures.

379
00:21:39,040 --> 00:21:43,500
Miners only made the decoration of the chapel.

380
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:45,980
There was one miner working at the time, of course,

381
00:21:46,080 --> 00:21:48,320
when one had finished and never started to work,

382
00:21:48,420 --> 00:21:49,440
then another one.

383
00:21:49,441 --> 00:21:52,940
but it took them 67 years to make all the figures,

384
00:21:53,040 --> 00:21:55,180
reliefs we have in the chapel.

385
00:21:55,280 --> 00:21:57,380
The Royal Wieliczka Salt Mines in Poland

386
00:21:57,480 --> 00:21:59,280
is one of the most extraordinary places

387
00:21:59,380 --> 00:22:00,880
anyone could ever visit.

388
00:22:00,980 --> 00:22:04,280
Miles and miles of salt mines, gorgeous black salt,

389
00:22:04,380 --> 00:22:08,800
and the city of Wieliczka is on top of the mines.

390
00:22:08,900 --> 00:22:12,400
The Wieliczka Salt Mine dates back to the Middle Ages

391
00:22:12,500 --> 00:22:17,900
and plunges as deep as 1,100 feet below the Earth's surface.

392
00:22:17,901 --> 00:22:24,700
125 miles of tunnels link 2,040 individual chambers.

393
00:22:24,800 --> 00:22:28,640
Over the past 700 years, miners have extracted more than 50

394
00:22:28,740 --> 00:22:30,440
million tons of rock salt.

395
00:22:33,740 --> 00:22:37,780
Since the 17th century, tourists have flocked to this mesmerizing

396
00:22:37,880 --> 00:22:42,120
underworld to view its subterranean pools, intricate

397
00:22:42,220 --> 00:22:47,900
networks of structural supports, and remarkable salt art.

398
00:22:47,901 --> 00:22:50,900
The United Nations has placed this humble salt mine on its

399
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,800
world heritage list as one of the cultural treasures

400
00:22:53,900 --> 00:22:55,700
of mankind.

401
00:22:55,800 --> 00:22:59,640
You can almost see thousands of miners working here,

402
00:22:59,740 --> 00:23:01,440
ages of our history.

403
00:23:01,540 --> 00:23:03,040
And it is very important, I think,

404
00:23:03,140 --> 00:23:05,960
not only for the Polish people, but for people from all over

405
00:23:06,060 --> 00:23:08,460
the world.

406
00:23:08,560 --> 00:23:10,260
The salt deposits beneath

407
00:23:10,360 --> 00:23:14,700
Wiolicka are residue from a Miocene-era primordial sea

408
00:23:14,800 --> 00:23:17,900
that evaporated 20 million years ago.

409
00:23:17,901 --> 00:23:22,900
In the 13th century AD, villagers

410
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:26,140
began digging into and mining the salt bed.

411
00:23:26,240 --> 00:23:29,140
The story of how the Vyelichka mine developed from there

412
00:23:29,240 --> 00:23:30,640
is shrouded in legend.

413
00:23:30,740 --> 00:23:33,240
It is a legend about the Princess Kinga,

414
00:23:33,340 --> 00:23:35,060
princess from Hungary.

415
00:23:35,160 --> 00:23:37,620
She married the Polish prince, and she

416
00:23:37,720 --> 00:23:39,460
decided to give us a present.

417
00:23:39,560 --> 00:23:41,060
She took her engagement ring.

418
00:23:41,160 --> 00:23:43,300
She threw it to the salt mine in Hungary.

419
00:23:43,400 --> 00:23:46,000
It was a sign that she took that mine in possession.

420
00:23:46,001 --> 00:23:48,900
And when she came to Poland, she ordered Polish miners

421
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:50,740
to start digging in our town.

422
00:23:50,840 --> 00:23:53,600
They found salt, and in the first block of salt,

423
00:23:53,700 --> 00:23:55,740
they found her ring.

424
00:23:55,840 --> 00:23:58,640
The salt was traveling underground following Kinga

425
00:23:58,740 --> 00:24:00,540
to her husband's country.

426
00:24:00,640 --> 00:24:03,020
That's the legend.

427
00:24:03,120 --> 00:24:06,060
In the Middle Ages, working conditions in salt mines

428
00:24:06,160 --> 00:24:08,920
were extremely harsh, especially for the first

429
00:24:09,020 --> 00:24:11,060
Violiczka miners.

430
00:24:11,160 --> 00:24:14,360
During the Middle Ages, Europe was the place

431
00:24:14,361 --> 00:24:19,389
was the place of almost mutual permanent wars.

432
00:24:19,390 --> 00:24:24,200
During these wars it was easy to get slaves.

433
00:24:24,840 --> 00:24:30,283
And these slaves were put to the industry. And what was the industry in this time?

434
00:24:30,284 --> 00:24:31,080
In this time

435
00:24:31,081 --> 00:24:37,631
the main industry was agriculture and mining operations at Vieliczka.

436
00:24:37,632 --> 00:24:39,720
So they put the slaves

437
00:24:39,721 --> 00:24:45,020
to Vyelichka mine and order them to work as much as possible,

438
00:24:45,120 --> 00:24:47,720
probably to death.

439
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:49,960
In the 14th century, free miners

440
00:24:50,060 --> 00:24:51,960
began working in Vyelichka.

441
00:24:52,060 --> 00:24:55,060
But they didn't have it much easier.

442
00:24:55,160 --> 00:24:57,440
Using tools of wood and iron, it

443
00:24:57,540 --> 00:25:02,380
took a team of three miners a full month to tunnel nine feet.

444
00:25:02,480 --> 00:25:05,020
Many miners spent their entire lives clearing salt

445
00:25:05,120 --> 00:25:05,920
from the same chamber.

446
00:25:05,921 --> 00:25:10,920
As the mine deepened, engineers faced the growing challenge

447
00:25:11,020 --> 00:25:13,920
of transporting workers to the bottom and salt

448
00:25:14,020 --> 00:25:15,660
to the surface.

449
00:25:15,760 --> 00:25:17,800
Here you can see the machine which

450
00:25:17,900 --> 00:25:20,700
was used to transport the miners, the horses, and the salt

451
00:25:20,800 --> 00:25:24,800
from the 18th century before the age of elevators.

452
00:25:24,900 --> 00:25:26,340
Look how big it is.

453
00:25:26,440 --> 00:25:29,280
It is also very old and very well preserved.

454
00:25:29,380 --> 00:25:31,520
It is because of the salt.

455
00:25:31,521 --> 00:25:36,320
It's the first system of elevator in the salt mine,

456
00:25:36,420 --> 00:25:42,660
operated by eight horses, which pulled around this capstan

457
00:25:42,760 --> 00:25:45,060
in both directions.

458
00:25:45,160 --> 00:25:52,280
This capstan was connected with shaft by very thick ropes.

459
00:25:52,380 --> 00:25:55,440
The ropes wound around massive wooden pulleys,

460
00:25:55,540 --> 00:25:58,980
which could lift salt blocks weighing two tons from the mine.

461
00:25:58,981 --> 00:26:02,220
It was so many years ago, it's still

462
00:26:02,320 --> 00:26:08,220
amazing how clever were those people using such tools

463
00:26:08,320 --> 00:26:11,220
made mainly of wood.

464
00:26:11,320 --> 00:26:13,220
Like all salt mines in the Middle Ages,

465
00:26:13,320 --> 00:26:15,220
the Olitschka was fraught with danger.

466
00:26:18,040 --> 00:26:20,940
The greatest hazard was explosive methane gas,

467
00:26:21,040 --> 00:26:23,780
released during the mining process.

468
00:26:23,880 --> 00:26:26,640
It would accumulate in crevices near the ceilings.

469
00:26:28,980 --> 00:26:33,980
The miners most at risk were the so-called penitents, who used

470
00:26:33,981 --> 00:26:36,980
long torches to burn off the pockets of gas.

471
00:26:38,980 --> 00:26:41,980
They earned the nickname because of the way they stretched out on

472
00:26:41,981 --> 00:26:44,980
remote ledges, as if appealing to God.

473
00:26:49,980 --> 00:26:53,980
Vyelichka's dangers compelled miners to fashion the religious

474
00:26:53,981 --> 00:26:57,980
icons, figures, and chapels that adorn the mine.

475
00:26:57,981 --> 00:27:02,380
In 1689, workers began seeking the divine protection

476
00:27:02,480 --> 00:27:06,380
of the mine's patron saint in daily services.

477
00:27:06,480 --> 00:27:09,520
The Blessed Kinga Chapel is the largest of three.

478
00:27:09,620 --> 00:27:16,540
It stretches 180 feet long, 60 feet wide, and 50 feet high.

479
00:27:16,640 --> 00:27:22,380
It endures as a unique religious and artistic masterpiece.

480
00:27:22,480 --> 00:27:24,740
The floor is really beautiful.

481
00:27:24,741 --> 00:27:32,340
really beautiful it looks like marble but it is salt polished by our visitors and not only the

482
00:27:32,341 --> 00:27:38,980
floor is made out of salt but also walls all the figures and all reliefs chandeliers in the blessed

483
00:27:38,981 --> 00:27:44,260
kinga chapel are decorated with the crystal salt from our mind we have two chambers full of very

484
00:27:44,261 --> 00:27:51,220
big crystals very symmetrical and so the miners decided to decorate the chandeliers with them

485
00:27:51,221 --> 00:27:54,720
And the crystals in the chandelier are translucent.

486
00:27:54,721 --> 00:27:59,660
So they illuminate very beautifully.

487
00:27:59,661 --> 00:28:02,860
Beauty of a different sort at Violichka lurks in chambers

488
00:28:02,861 --> 00:28:05,400
off limits to tourists.

489
00:28:05,401 --> 00:28:11,780
Salt marvels crafted not by man, but by nature.

490
00:28:11,781 --> 00:28:13,740
Violichka is not the only salt mine

491
00:28:13,741 --> 00:28:15,720
with a subterranean chapel.

492
00:28:15,721 --> 00:28:18,680
Colombia's Zipakira Mine contains a cathedral that

493
00:28:18,681 --> 00:28:23,080
that can hold 1,800 people and features a version of Michelangelo's

494
00:28:23,081 --> 00:28:25,420
creation of man carved in salt.

495
00:28:30,020 --> 00:28:32,560
At locales around the world, nature

496
00:28:32,561 --> 00:28:36,240
crafts salt like an artist molds clay.

497
00:28:36,241 --> 00:28:39,140
Southern California's Mono Lake features salt towers

498
00:28:39,141 --> 00:28:42,940
known as tufas. Some reach as high as 30 feet

499
00:28:42,941 --> 00:28:47,100
and date back 900 years.

500
00:28:47,101 --> 00:28:50,340
On Bolivia's Alta Plano, the plateau where the Andes

501
00:28:50,341 --> 00:28:55,000
are at their widest, lies the Uyuni Salt Flat, the largest

502
00:28:55,001 --> 00:28:57,180
salt bed in the world.

503
00:28:57,181 --> 00:29:00,180
The salt layer penetrates down more than 30 feet

504
00:29:00,181 --> 00:29:04,880
and weighs a staggering 64 billion tons.

505
00:29:04,881 --> 00:29:08,320
And some of salt's most intriguing natural formations

506
00:29:08,321 --> 00:29:11,020
lurk in Poland's Wiolicka salt mine

507
00:29:11,021 --> 00:29:15,960
at the end of obscure passageways off limits to tourists.

508
00:29:15,961 --> 00:29:20,500
We would like to show other parts of the mine to the public,

509
00:29:20,501 --> 00:29:22,500
but it is dangerous.

510
00:29:22,501 --> 00:29:26,100
We have carbon monoxide methane in this mine.

511
00:29:26,101 --> 00:29:27,900
Some corridors are very slow, and they

512
00:29:27,901 --> 00:29:30,040
are going to collapse in the future.

513
00:29:30,041 --> 00:29:34,260
So unfortunately, the real mine is closed for the visitors.

514
00:29:38,800 --> 00:29:44,980
The real mine is an untamed and captivating environment,

515
00:29:44,981 --> 00:29:47,380
the surprisingly varied forms of salt.

516
00:29:50,380 --> 00:29:53,020
Strangely wavy chambers like this one

517
00:29:53,120 --> 00:29:55,420
were once filled from top to bottom with water.

518
00:29:58,720 --> 00:30:03,000
The water was siphoned out and crystallized into salt,

519
00:30:03,100 --> 00:30:04,640
leaving a desolate moonscape.

520
00:30:08,040 --> 00:30:10,980
This barely upright cavern, accessible only

521
00:30:11,080 --> 00:30:14,980
through a hole in a wall, looks like a trash heap.

522
00:30:14,981 --> 00:30:18,020
But light converts a salt-soaked piece of wood

523
00:30:18,120 --> 00:30:22,080
into a raft of gleaming jewels.

524
00:30:22,180 --> 00:30:26,020
In addition to the many unexpected sights of Vyelichka,

525
00:30:26,120 --> 00:30:29,020
there are also unexpected sounds.

526
00:30:29,120 --> 00:30:32,860
Sometimes our visitors are really surprised when they

527
00:30:32,960 --> 00:30:38,840
are in the corridor and suddenly they hear music.

528
00:30:38,940 --> 00:30:43,940
The band is very old, and we have almost only miners

529
00:30:43,941 --> 00:30:47,940
and retired miners in our band.

530
00:30:48,040 --> 00:30:51,640
But acoustics in the Weimar chamber is wonderful,

531
00:30:51,740 --> 00:30:56,880
so they usually play here on the second level.

532
00:30:56,980 --> 00:31:01,000
The Wiolicka Salt Mine Band was formed in 1830.

533
00:31:01,100 --> 00:31:04,500
It's been entertaining visitors here for nearly two centuries.

534
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:11,840
It harkens back to the mine's golden age of tourism.

535
00:31:11,841 --> 00:31:15,080
Kings and commoners once flocked to the saltworks to dance

536
00:31:15,180 --> 00:31:18,640
in enormous ballrooms and dine in banquet halls carved

537
00:31:18,740 --> 00:31:19,940
from pure salt.

538
00:31:22,080 --> 00:31:25,580
Royal guests could tour the mine on a subterranean railway

539
00:31:25,680 --> 00:31:27,780
in velvet upholstered cars.

540
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:33,500
Visitors could also explore the wonders of Vyelichka by boat,

541
00:31:33,600 --> 00:31:36,900
rowing through brine grottos and lakes like this one,

542
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:38,500
along a system of canals.

543
00:31:38,501 --> 00:31:45,000
canals. Officials banned boating less than a century ago, when

544
00:31:45,001 --> 00:31:49,000
three Swiss revelers capsized and drowned, unable to escape from

545
00:31:49,001 --> 00:31:51,500
under their canoe in the heavy brine.

546
00:31:55,500 --> 00:31:58,500
Not everyone who ventured within the Violichka mine came

547
00:31:58,501 --> 00:32:03,500
willingly. Toward the end of the Second

548
00:32:03,501 --> 00:32:07,500
World War, the Nazis herded Jewish captives here.

549
00:32:07,501 --> 00:32:10,700
The Germans' plan was to convert this towering chamber into an

550
00:32:10,701 --> 00:32:14,500
airplane parts factory in which the Jews would labor.

551
00:32:16,500 --> 00:32:20,500
The war ended before production began, but not before the Nazis

552
00:32:20,501 --> 00:32:24,500
led some 2,000 Jews out of the mine and on a death march to

553
00:32:24,501 --> 00:32:26,500
concentration camps in Czechoslovakia.

554
00:32:29,500 --> 00:32:32,500
A Star of David, etched by miners after the war,

555
00:32:32,501 --> 00:32:34,500
memorializes those captives.

556
00:32:37,500 --> 00:32:41,900
Today, this unique underworld so rich with history

557
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:44,100
threatens to become history itself.

558
00:32:46,940 --> 00:32:50,180
All nine levels of the mine are held in place by pillars and

559
00:32:50,280 --> 00:32:53,080
logs, which are fighting a losing battle against the

560
00:32:53,180 --> 00:32:54,880
immense weight of the Earth.

561
00:32:54,980 --> 00:32:59,860
Some of them are broken like as a match.

562
00:32:59,960 --> 00:33:05,120
What destroyed this very thick wood so easily?

563
00:33:05,121 --> 00:33:08,080
It's the pressure of earth.

564
00:33:08,180 --> 00:33:11,620
The more cavities we create and support,

565
00:33:11,720 --> 00:33:16,860
the more logs of wood are broken.

566
00:33:16,960 --> 00:33:19,760
Relentless water seepage and crumbling supports

567
00:33:19,860 --> 00:33:21,360
also imperil the mine.

568
00:33:23,580 --> 00:33:28,820
A flood in 1992 swamped its fourth level, 566 feet

569
00:33:28,920 --> 00:33:31,720
underground, which is still inaccessible.

570
00:33:35,120 --> 00:33:38,080
Above, in the village of Vyeliczka,

571
00:33:38,081 --> 00:33:40,160
periodic sinkholes remind residents

572
00:33:40,161 --> 00:33:43,620
that they live on very shaky ground.

573
00:33:43,621 --> 00:33:47,660
Seven years ago, a part of our railway trucks subsided

574
00:33:47,661 --> 00:33:50,160
because of the flood in the mine,

575
00:33:50,161 --> 00:33:51,660
and we don't have a railway station

576
00:33:51,661 --> 00:33:53,580
in the center of the town anymore.

577
00:33:53,581 --> 00:33:55,280
A train cannot get there.

578
00:33:55,281 --> 00:33:57,580
Almost everyone living in Vyeliczka

579
00:33:57,581 --> 00:34:01,080
has a chamber or a corridor under his house.

580
00:34:01,081 --> 00:34:04,820
So we can all wake up in the mine one day.

581
00:34:08,820 --> 00:34:11,420
Beyond the threats to the mine's structural integrity

582
00:34:11,520 --> 00:34:15,320
are those to its unique collection of art.

583
00:34:15,420 --> 00:34:18,300
Humidity, especially near the windy shafts,

584
00:34:18,400 --> 00:34:21,200
has eroded some of Vyelichka's salt sculptures,

585
00:34:21,300 --> 00:34:22,740
which are simply melting away.

586
00:34:31,080 --> 00:34:33,420
Ironically, while the mine itself is ailing,

587
00:34:33,520 --> 00:34:35,720
it's providing a cure for respiratory patients

588
00:34:35,820 --> 00:34:39,820
from around Europe who come here to take in the salty air.

589
00:34:42,600 --> 00:34:47,200
Of all the sites in Vyelichka, this might be the strangest,

590
00:34:47,300 --> 00:34:49,740
an 80-bed sanatorium on the fifth level,

591
00:34:49,840 --> 00:34:52,200
650 feet underground.

592
00:34:52,300 --> 00:34:55,500
We have many people suffering from asthma, bronchitis,

593
00:34:55,600 --> 00:34:58,200
and allergies, resting and breathing.

594
00:34:58,201 --> 00:35:01,700
It is very healthy for them to spend some time underground

595
00:35:01,800 --> 00:35:04,440
in the salt mine, because an air in the salt mine

596
00:35:04,540 --> 00:35:07,280
is very clean, almost free of bacteria

597
00:35:07,380 --> 00:35:10,680
and completely free of fungi.

598
00:35:10,780 --> 00:35:15,320
Violetschka isn't the only salt mine with a few surprises.

599
00:35:15,420 --> 00:35:19,180
During World War II, the Nazis converted one salt mine

600
00:35:19,280 --> 00:35:23,320
into their own version of Fort Knox.

601
00:35:23,420 --> 00:35:26,320
In 1998, salt helped advance the search

602
00:35:26,321 --> 00:35:28,460
for extraterrestrial life.

603
00:35:28,560 --> 00:35:31,360
Scientists examining a meteorite that fell in Texas

604
00:35:31,460 --> 00:35:35,500
discovered water preserved inside purple salt crystals.

605
00:35:35,600 --> 00:35:38,560
It marked the first time water was found in an object from

606
00:35:38,660 --> 00:35:39,360
space.

607
00:35:42,900 --> 00:35:45,440
Although we may think of salt as a kind of food,

608
00:35:45,540 --> 00:35:47,680
over the centuries, it's played a more important role

609
00:35:47,780 --> 00:35:50,380
as a food preservative.

610
00:35:50,480 --> 00:35:53,340
Before refrigeration, people packed fish, meat,

611
00:35:53,440 --> 00:35:55,140
and other perishables in salt.

612
00:35:55,141 --> 00:35:56,840
as a safeguard against famine.

613
00:36:00,440 --> 00:36:02,840
In the 20th century, as we sought to preserve goods

614
00:36:02,940 --> 00:36:05,880
beyond food, we found an ideal solution in the mines

615
00:36:05,980 --> 00:36:07,080
where salt originated.

616
00:36:10,280 --> 00:36:12,460
The temperature is very even in a salt mine,

617
00:36:12,560 --> 00:36:16,800
and the humidity is very even, which is very important.

618
00:36:16,900 --> 00:36:20,100
645 feet beneath the Kansas prairie,

619
00:36:20,200 --> 00:36:22,800
a salt mine doubles as an enormous warehouse.

620
00:36:22,801 --> 00:36:24,800
warehouse.

621
00:36:24,900 --> 00:36:28,800
28 acres of storage space are entombed in a salt deposit 100

622
00:36:28,900 --> 00:36:34,640
miles long, 40 miles wide, and 325 feet thick.

623
00:36:34,740 --> 00:36:38,040
Clients store everything here, from 19th century newspapers

624
00:36:38,140 --> 00:36:42,020
to tax records and secret government documents.

625
00:36:42,120 --> 00:36:43,780
One of the facility's greatest treasures

626
00:36:43,880 --> 00:36:47,000
comes from Hollywood.

627
00:36:47,100 --> 00:36:49,960
Reel after reel of original film negatives,

628
00:36:49,961 --> 00:36:51,960
from The Wizard of Oz to Star Wars.

629
00:36:55,100 --> 00:36:58,900
Among the first to exploit salt mines as long-term warehouses

630
00:36:59,000 --> 00:37:02,100
were the Nazis.

631
00:37:02,200 --> 00:37:05,100
Near the end of World War II, the Allies discovered

632
00:37:05,200 --> 00:37:07,680
they had been using mines throughout the Third Reich

633
00:37:07,780 --> 00:37:11,080
to store the plunder of conquest.

634
00:37:11,180 --> 00:37:12,920
The Nazis stashed their greatest treasures

635
00:37:13,020 --> 00:37:17,720
in a salt mine in the village of Merkers, southwest of Berlin,

636
00:37:17,721 --> 00:37:21,560
1,200 feet underground, the maze of tunnels and chambers

637
00:37:21,660 --> 00:37:26,160
made for an exceptional hiding place.

638
00:37:26,260 --> 00:37:30,660
In March 1945, American troops advancing through Merkers

639
00:37:30,760 --> 00:37:34,200
discovered the hideaway, but only because of a chance

640
00:37:34,300 --> 00:37:36,380
encounter with the locals.

641
00:37:36,480 --> 00:37:38,680
There were two ladies walking along the road,

642
00:37:38,780 --> 00:37:41,040
and some MPs gave them a ride, and they said,

643
00:37:41,140 --> 00:37:43,440
oh, well, have you found the mine with the gold yet?

644
00:37:43,540 --> 00:37:46,020
And so that was the first word they had.

645
00:37:46,021 --> 00:37:48,520
And then after a little while, some soldiers turned up

646
00:37:48,620 --> 00:37:52,120
with helmets full of gold coins.

647
00:37:52,220 --> 00:37:54,700
After dynamiting their way into shaft number three

648
00:37:54,800 --> 00:37:56,700
of the Merker's mine, the Americans

649
00:37:56,800 --> 00:38:00,340
were stunned by what they found.

650
00:38:00,440 --> 00:38:03,640
The entire gold reserve of the Third Reich.

651
00:38:03,740 --> 00:38:08,240
100 tons of gold bullion, countless sacks of gold coins,

652
00:38:08,340 --> 00:38:10,640
and bales of international currency,

653
00:38:10,740 --> 00:38:14,420
including $2 million American dollars, 100 million French

654
00:38:14,421 --> 00:38:16,420
and 3 billion German marks.

655
00:38:20,420 --> 00:38:24,420
An Austrian salt mine in the tiny village of Alt-Aussee

656
00:38:24,421 --> 00:38:28,420
served as a hideout for a different kind of Nazi treasure,

657
00:38:28,421 --> 00:38:31,420
Adolf Hitler's personal collection of paintings

658
00:38:31,421 --> 00:38:33,420
and other works of art.

659
00:38:33,421 --> 00:38:36,420
The Germans had systematically pilfered most of them

660
00:38:36,421 --> 00:38:39,420
from the European nations they'd conquered.

661
00:38:39,421 --> 00:38:42,420
The people who'd gone to check it out in the first place

662
00:38:42,421 --> 00:38:45,220
had found a little chapel there, and in the chapel

663
00:38:45,320 --> 00:38:47,860
were a bunch of oil paintings which had been hanging there

664
00:38:47,960 --> 00:38:51,360
since 1933, and this was 1945.

665
00:38:51,460 --> 00:38:53,560
And they were in just perfect condition,

666
00:38:53,660 --> 00:38:55,760
so they realized that this was a very good place

667
00:38:55,860 --> 00:38:59,640
to store works of art.

668
00:38:59,740 --> 00:39:01,380
We're housed in the salt mine

669
00:39:01,480 --> 00:39:03,840
were such masterworks as Vermeer's self-portrait

670
00:39:03,940 --> 00:39:09,780
in the studio, the Ghent Altarpiece by Van Eyck,

671
00:39:09,781 --> 00:39:14,780
and the Bruges Madonna by Michelangelo.

672
00:39:14,781 --> 00:39:18,780
The Nazis had stolen the Madonna from Brussels in a nighttime raid

673
00:39:18,781 --> 00:39:22,780
and transported it across Europe wrapped in a mattress.

674
00:39:22,781 --> 00:39:26,780
The sculpture was still in the mattress when American soldiers

675
00:39:26,781 --> 00:39:29,780
found it in Alt-Aussee.

676
00:39:29,781 --> 00:39:31,780
But it wasn't the only treasure stored there in a way that

677
00:39:31,781 --> 00:39:33,780
belied its pricelessness.

678
00:39:33,781 --> 00:39:37,780
The Gantt altarpiece was taken apart in separate panels

679
00:39:37,781 --> 00:39:41,120
and sort of sitting on a little wooden scaffolding.

680
00:39:41,220 --> 00:39:45,520
But for the officers going into this Aladdin's cave,

681
00:39:45,620 --> 00:39:49,520
it was just a remarkable and very emotional experience,

682
00:39:49,620 --> 00:39:52,420
because these were things they'd studied all their lives.

683
00:39:52,520 --> 00:39:55,940
And here they were in this very peculiar situation.

684
00:39:56,040 --> 00:39:59,440
Make that a precarious situation.

685
00:39:59,540 --> 00:40:02,000
The precious works of art were nearly destroyed

686
00:40:02,100 --> 00:40:04,100
in the last days of the war.

687
00:40:04,200 --> 00:40:06,480
Just days before the Allies arrived,

688
00:40:06,481 --> 00:40:09,780
arrived, Nazi soldiers placed 100-pound bombs

689
00:40:09,880 --> 00:40:11,720
throughout the mine.

690
00:40:11,820 --> 00:40:14,680
But German miners and curators saved the day,

691
00:40:14,780 --> 00:40:19,220
detonating smaller decoy bombs to deceive the SS.

692
00:40:19,320 --> 00:40:22,860
Later, demolition experts removed the real bombs.

693
00:40:22,960 --> 00:40:25,200
It seemed to take forever to get them out

694
00:40:25,300 --> 00:40:27,160
of these long, long tunnels.

695
00:40:27,260 --> 00:40:29,640
And they put them in a big heap outside of the mine.

696
00:40:29,740 --> 00:40:31,680
It was quite an amusing picture of them later on,

697
00:40:31,780 --> 00:40:34,500
all sitting on these bombs.

698
00:40:34,501 --> 00:40:37,740
But they did save both the mine and the incredible treasures

699
00:40:37,840 --> 00:40:38,740
that were inside.

700
00:40:42,040 --> 00:40:44,640
In the many decades since World War II,

701
00:40:44,740 --> 00:40:47,780
salt mines have continued to prove invaluable as mass storage

702
00:40:47,880 --> 00:40:50,140
facilities.

703
00:40:50,240 --> 00:40:54,320
Energy providers use them as gargantuan fuel tanks.

704
00:40:54,420 --> 00:40:58,060
For example, we have our strategic petroleum reserve

705
00:40:58,160 --> 00:40:59,720
in salt caverns.

706
00:40:59,721 --> 00:41:04,720
Many areas have excavated caverns for the storage

707
00:41:04,820 --> 00:41:10,100
of natural gas, which is put under pressure in those.

708
00:41:10,200 --> 00:41:12,560
Deep below the New Mexico desert,

709
00:41:12,660 --> 00:41:15,540
a salt mine functions as America's solution to one of its

710
00:41:15,640 --> 00:41:17,040
most critical storage problems.

711
00:41:20,500 --> 00:41:24,180
Since 1999, Department of Energy transport trucks

712
00:41:24,280 --> 00:41:26,940
have been delivering containers of nuclear waste

713
00:41:26,941 --> 00:41:30,240
to this waste dump known as WIP.

714
00:41:30,340 --> 00:41:33,920
WIP stands for the Waste Isolation Pilot Plant.

715
00:41:34,020 --> 00:41:40,960
It was a facility constructed to safely dispose of plutonium

716
00:41:41,060 --> 00:41:42,860
contaminated nuclear waste.

717
00:41:45,720 --> 00:41:48,560
Workers lower the waste more than 2,100 feet

718
00:41:48,660 --> 00:41:51,400
to the base of the mine, where it will remain toxic

719
00:41:51,500 --> 00:41:53,260
for the next quarter million years.

720
00:41:53,261 --> 00:41:59,300
Nine miles of tunnels link disposal rooms 13 feet high,

721
00:41:59,400 --> 00:42:03,140
33 feet wide, and 300 feet long.

722
00:42:03,240 --> 00:42:06,780
Salt is particularly attractive because it

723
00:42:06,880 --> 00:42:09,240
tends to act like a plastic, so it

724
00:42:09,340 --> 00:42:13,080
creeps over geologic time, thousands of years.

725
00:42:13,180 --> 00:42:14,920
And if there's a fracture opening,

726
00:42:15,020 --> 00:42:17,580
like the rooms we would put waste in at WIPP,

727
00:42:17,680 --> 00:42:20,780
the salt would close in and basically recrystallize

728
00:42:20,880 --> 00:42:21,960
around the waste.

729
00:42:21,961 --> 00:42:23,200
waste.

730
00:42:23,300 --> 00:42:26,460
This natural process, which takes eons to occur,

731
00:42:26,560 --> 00:42:28,360
will safely seal the nuclear waste

732
00:42:28,460 --> 00:42:31,940
in a permanent hard cocoon.

733
00:42:32,040 --> 00:42:34,700
Still, WIPP officials acknowledge the danger

734
00:42:34,800 --> 00:42:37,100
to distant generations who might discover

735
00:42:37,200 --> 00:42:39,920
the lethal deposits.

736
00:42:40,020 --> 00:42:43,380
The Department of Energy faces an unprecedented dilemma,

737
00:42:43,480 --> 00:42:45,080
how to send a warning to people who

738
00:42:45,180 --> 00:42:48,680
won't be born for hundreds of thousands of years.

739
00:42:48,780 --> 00:42:50,620
We're not going to walk away from that facility

740
00:42:50,621 --> 00:42:53,080
when we're done filling it up, saying, well,

741
00:42:53,081 --> 00:42:56,060
I hope everyone just stays away.

742
00:42:56,061 --> 00:42:58,920
There will be a sophisticated system of records

743
00:42:58,921 --> 00:43:01,260
and archives throughout the country,

744
00:43:01,261 --> 00:43:06,860
as well as markers on site that we will do our best

745
00:43:06,861 --> 00:43:09,640
in as many languages as we think makes sense

746
00:43:09,641 --> 00:43:15,180
to warn people about the hazards of what lies underneath.

747
00:43:15,181 --> 00:43:18,380
Thus far, proposed warning markers for the toxic salt mine

748
00:43:18,381 --> 00:43:25,760
include a field of spikes, a sequence of grimacing masks,

749
00:43:25,860 --> 00:43:27,260
and a landscape of thorns.

750
00:43:31,000 --> 00:43:33,520
If salt does prove to be a reliable repository

751
00:43:33,620 --> 00:43:36,760
for nuclear waste, it'll be a fitting new role

752
00:43:36,860 --> 00:43:39,660
for a mineral that humans have come to depend on in so many

753
00:43:39,760 --> 00:43:41,100
ways.

754
00:43:41,200 --> 00:43:46,080
Salt, the ultimate preservative, has safeguarded our health,

755
00:43:46,081 --> 00:43:53,880
our food, our gold, and now, quite possibly, our future,

756
00:43:53,980 --> 00:43:57,100
a future in which innovators promise to conceive new uses

757
00:43:57,200 --> 00:43:59,920
for this humble and versatile mineral,

758
00:44:00,020 --> 00:44:02,960
and for the spectacular minds from which it comes.

